+      <div class="journal-body">
+        <p class="journal-eyebrow">Roaster's Journal</p>
+        <h2 id="journal-title">From Hillside to Hopper: How We Source Our Beans</h2>
+        <p class="journal-byline">Notes from our head roaster</p>
+
+        <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+          <p>Every bag of coffee we roast starts with a relationship, not a transaction. Long before a single bean reaches our drum roaster, we've spent months, sometimes years, getting to know the people who grow it. We buy the majority of our green coffee directly from a small circle of farms and cooperatives in Colombia, Ethiopia, Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we visit each of them in person at least once every harvest cycle.</p>
+
+          <h3>Starting at the farm</h3>
+          <p>Our sourcing trips are timed to the harvest. We walk the rows with producers, talk about what the weather did to this year's cherries, and taste coffee straight off the patio drying beds. Those conversations shape what we buy. If a farm in Huila tells us a late rain pushed their harvest back, we adjust our roasting calendar instead of asking them to rush the picking. Ripe cherries, hand-selected over several passes, are the single biggest factor in a sweet, clean cup, and we're willing to wait for them.</p>
+
+          <h3>Paying for quality, not commodity prices</h3>
+          <p>The commodity "C" price for coffee swings wildly and often falls below what it actually costs a family to grow it. We don't use it as our benchmark. Instead, we agree on prices with each producer before the harvest based on their real costs and the quality they're aiming for, and we've committed to paying at least 50% above the Fairtrade minimum on every lot. We publish those numbers each year in our transparency report, because we think you deserve to know where your money goes.</p>
+
+          <h3>Cupping, cupping, and more cupping</h3>
+          <p>When pre-shipment samples arrive at our roastery, our team sits down for blind cuppings. We score each sample for sweetness, acidity, body, and clarity, and we reject anything that doesn't meet our standards, even from long-time partners. When that happens, we share detailed feedback so we can work together on processing or drying the following season. Some of our best coffees today came from lots we turned down three years ago.</p>
+
+          <h3>Shipping and storage</h3>
+          <p>Green coffee is a living agricultural product, and it fades if it's treated poorly. Our beans travel in hermetically sealed GrainPro bags inside traditional jute sacks, which keeps moisture stable during the long ocean crossing. Once they land, they go into a climate-controlled warehouse a few miles from our shop, and we only bring in what we can roast within about six months of arrival.</p>
+
+          <h3>Roasting in small batches</h3>
+          <p>Finally, the beans come home to our 15-kilo roaster. We develop a roast profile for each new lot, aiming to highlight what makes that particular farm special rather than covering it up with heavy roast flavors. We roast five days a week in small batches, so the coffee in your cup was almost always roasted within the past seven days. It's a slower, more expensive way to run a coffee shop, but every time a guest tells us they can taste the blueberry in our Ethiopian Guji, we know it's worth it.</p>
